 231 369C231 372 224 390 220 397C205 423 176 442 135 442C128 442 122 441 118 440C69 429 41 367 26 308C23 298 21 288 21 287C21 284 25 280 27 278L41 278C56 278 58 279 60 284C60 285 63 295 66 305C72 329 81 356 87 368C98 390 109 405 129 405C147 405 153 388 153 366C153 358 153 348 152 343C146 308 74 40 74 17C74-1 91-11 108-11Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-TEX-N-28\" d=\"M333-241C333-237 332-236 322-226C217-115 167 37 167 250C167 463 217 615 322 726C332 736 333 737 333 741C333 743 332 745 330 747C327 750 327 750 315 750L302 750L274 726C205 663 153 575 123 473C103 403 94 342 94 250C94 39 160-122 274-226L302-250L315-250C328-250 333-249 333-241Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-TEX-N-2B\" d=\"M56 250C56 239 62 234 70 230L369 230L369-68C373-76 378-82 389-82C400-82 405-76 409-68L409 230L707 230C715 234 722 239 722 250C722 261 715 266 707 270L409 270L409 568C405 576 399 583 389 583C379 583 374 575 370 570L369 420L369 270L70 270C62 266 56 261 56 250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-TEX-N-32\" d=\"M110 429C147 429 170 457 170 489C170 520 149 549 114 549C111 549 109 549 109 550C109 551 114 559 118 567C140 599 167 619 212 619C298 619 343 548 343 464C343 406 321 358 276 297C254 269 249 262 136 137C91 86 53 43 52 42C50 39 50 36 50 19L50 0L421 0L421 3C424 9 446 180 449 186L449 189L409 189L409 186C401 141 400 132 395 118C392 108 388 98 385 97C379 94 319 92 218 92L142 93L201 149C269 214 266 211 301 241C367 298 393 325 414 356C438 391 449 421 449 465C449 477 448 489 447 498C430 596 352 666 235 666C133 666 50 586 50 491C50 457 72 429 110 429Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-TEX-N-29\" d=\"M60 749C57 747 55 742 55 738C56 737 61 731 66 725C172 615 221 462 221 250C221 38 172-115 66-225C61-231 56-237 55-238C55-249 59-250 74-250L86-250L114-226C183-163 235-75 265 28C285 96 294 159 294 250C294 461 228 622 114 726L86 750L74 750C66 750 62 749 60 749Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-TEX-N-2234\" d=\"M273 411C273 377 299 351 333 351C367 351 393 377 393 411C393 444 366 471 334 471C299 471 273 445 273 411M143-22C143 13 118 38 84 38C49 38 24 13 24-22C24-57 48-82 83-82C118-82 143-57 143-22M583-82C618-82 643-57 643-22C643 12 618 38 584 38C549 38 524 13 524-22C524-57 548-82 583-82Z\"></path></defs></svg>","mathFallback":false,"description":"JEE Main's 2019 JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot Paper's All Questions with solutions provider ExamSIDE.Com","paperTitle":"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot","nextq":"ov1PKjoChpAPDQ3efSBqx","prevq":"nloFDKC2WEfANEZUek76g","questions":[{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","exam":"jee-main","subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","chapter":"coordination-compounds","year":2019,"paperTitle":"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"languages":["en","hi"],"difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":"2019-04-09 14:30","question":{"en":{"content":"The correct statements among I to III are :<br>\n(I) Valence bond theory cannot explain the\ncolor exhibited by transition metal\ncomplexes.<br>\n(II) Valence bond theory can predict\nquantitatively the magnetic properties of\ntranstition metal complexes.<br>\n(III) Valence bond theory cannot distinguish\nligands as weak and strong field ones.","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(II) and (III) only"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(I) and (II) only"},{"identifier":"C","content":"(I), (II) and (III)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"(I) and (III) only"}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>To determine which statements are correct, let's analyze each one individually in the context of <strong>Valence Bond Theory (VBT)</strong> as it applies to transition metal complexes.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Statement (I):</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>\"Valence bond theory cannot explain the color exhibited by transition metal complexes.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Analysis:</strong></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Color in Transition Metal Complexes:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>The colors of transition metal complexes arise from <strong>electronic transitions</strong> between different energy levels of the d-orbitals, specifically <strong>d-d transitions</strong>.</p></p>\n<p><p>These transitions occur when an electron absorbs light energy and moves from a lower-energy d-orbital to a higher-energy d-orbital.</p></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Valence Bond Theory Limitations:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>VBT focuses on the <strong>hybridization of atomic orbitals</strong> to form covalent bonds.</p></p>\n<p><p>It does not account for the splitting of d-orbitals into different energy levels in the presence of ligands (known as <strong>crystal field splitting</strong>).</p></p>\n<p><p>Therefore, VBT <strong>cannot explain the origin of color</strong> in these complexes because it doesn't address the electronic transitions responsible for color.</p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I) is correct.</strong></p>\n\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Statement (II):</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>\"Valence bond theory can predict quantitatively the magnetic properties of transition metal complexes.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Analysis:</strong></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Magnetic Properties:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>The magnetic behavior of a complex depends on the number of <strong>unpaired electrons</strong> in the metal ion.</p></p>\n<p><p>Quantitative prediction requires calculating the <strong>magnetic moment</strong>, often using the formula:</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.639ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"42.21ex\" height=\"2.851ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -977.5 18657 1260\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D707\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D707\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(880.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msqrt\" transform=\"translate(1936.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(0,67.5)\"><use data-c=\"221A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-SO-221A\"></use></g><g transform=\"translate(1020,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(600,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(989,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1811.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2811.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3311.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"3700.4\" height=\"60\" x=\"1020\" y=\"857.5\"></rect></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\" transform=\"translate(6657,0)\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\"> </text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\" transform=\"translate(7257,0)\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\">Bohr Magnetons (BM)</text></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>where <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.357ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 600 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D45B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is the number of unpaired electrons.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Valence Bond Theory Capabilities:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>VBT can provide a <strong>qualitative</strong> idea about the magnetic properties by indicating whether a complex is <strong>paramagnetic</strong> (unpaired electrons present) or <strong>diamagnetic</strong> (no unpaired electrons).</p></p>\n<p><p>However, VBT does not offer the tools to <strong>quantitatively</strong> predict the exact magnetic moment.</p></p>\n<p><p>Accurate quantitative predictions require more advanced theories like <strong>Crystal Field Theory (CFT)</strong> or <strong>Ligand Field Theory (LFT)</strong>.</p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (II) is incorrect.</strong></p>\n\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Statement (III):</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>\"Valence bond theory cannot distinguish ligands as weak and strong field ones.\"</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Analysis:</strong></p>\n\n<p><p><strong>Weak and Strong Field Ligands:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>Ligands are classified based on their ability to split the d-orbitals of the metal ion, influencing the pairing of electrons.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Strong field ligands</strong> cause a large splitting, often leading to low-spin complexes.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Weak field ligands</strong> cause small splitting, leading to high-spin complexes.</p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Valence Bond Theory Limitations:</strong></p></p>\n<p><p>VBT does not address the energy splitting of d-orbitals.</p></p>\n<p><p>It assumes that all bonds are formed via overlap of orbitals without considering the effect of ligands on d-orbital energies.</p></p>\n<p><p>Therefore, VBT <strong>cannot distinguish between weak and strong field ligands</strong> because it doesn't involve the spectrochemical series or orbital splitting concepts.</p></p>\n\n<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (III) is correct.</strong></p>\n\n<hr>\n<h3><strong>Final Answer:</strong></h3>\n\n<p><p><strong>Statements (I) and (III) are correct.</strong></p></p>\n<p><p><strong>Statement (II) is incorrect.</strong></p></p>\n\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Option D</p>\n<p><strong>(I) and (III) only</strong></p>"},"hi":{"content":"सही कथन I से III में से हैं :<br>\n(I) वैलेंस बॉन्ड सिद्धांत संक्रमण धातु संयुक्तों द्वारा प्रदर्शित रंग को समझा नहीं सकता।<br>\n(II) वैलेंस बॉन्ड सिद्धांत संक्रमण धातु संयुक्तों के चुंबकीय गुणों की मात्रा में भविष्यवाणी कर सकता है।<br>\n(III) वैलेंस बॉन्ड सिद्धांत कमजोर और मजबूत क्षेत्र वाले लिगेंडों में अंतर नहीं कर सकता।","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(II) और (III) केवल"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(I) और (II) केवल"},{"identifier":"C","content":"(I), (II) और (III)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"(I) और (III) केवल"}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":null,"comprehension":null,"direction":null}},"question_id":"EqgJ5MWVQbKw7AJZzYit0","permalink":"the-correct-statements-among-i-to-iii-are-i-valence-bond-jee-main-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-mhv8noghzxkm8kdc","updated_time":1566842850,"timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","topic":"warners-theory-and-valence-bond-theory","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":["multi_statements"],"yearKey":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","exam":"jee-main","subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","chapter":"d-and-f-block-elements","year":2019,"paperTitle":"JEE Main 2019 (Online) 9th April Evening Slot","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"languages":["en"],"difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":"2019-04-09 14:30","question":{"en":{"content":"The maximum number of possible oxidation\nstates of actinoides are shown by :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"nobelium (No) and lawrencium (Lr)"},{"identifier":"B","content":"actinium (Ac) and thorium (Th)"},{"identifier":"C","content":"neptunium (Np) and plutonium (Pu)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"berkelium (Bk) and californium (Cf)"}],"correct_options":["C"],"answer":null,"explanation":"Actinoid 'Th' shows  oxidation state = +4\n<br><br>Actinoid 'Ac' shows  oxidation state = +3\n<br><br>Actinoid 'Pu' shows  oxidation states = +3, +4, +5, +6, +7\n<br><br>Actinoid 'Np' shows  oxidation states = +3, +4, +5, +6, +7\n<br><br>Actinoid 'Bk' shows  oxidation states = +3, +4\n<br><br>Actinoid 'Cm' shows  oxidation states = +3, +4\n<br><br>Actinoid 'Lr' shows  oxidation states = +3\n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.509ex\" height=\"1.251ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -471 667 553\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2234\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2234\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Maximum oxidation state is shown by Np\nand Pu."},"hi":{"content":"एक्टिनाइड के संभावित ऑक्सीकरण अवस्थाओं की अधिकतम संख्या दिखाई जाती है द्वारा :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"नोबेलियम (No) और लॉरेंसियम (Lr)"},{"identifier":"B","content":"एक्टिनियम (Ac) और थोरियम (Th)"},{"identifier":"C","content":"नेप्टूनियम (Np) और प्लूटोनियम (Pu)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"बर्केलियम (Bk) और कैलिफ़ोर्नियम (Cf)"}],"correct_options":["C"],"answer":null,"explanation":null,"comprehension":null,"direction":null}},"question_id":"YZw5LxVmGvfP0XcP7o2Nb","permalink":"the-maximum-number-of-possible-oxidation-states-of-actinoide-jee-main-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-1wvavrmvva85pqsv","updated_time":1566766877,"timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"jee-main-2019-online-9th-april-evening-slot","topic":"inner-transition-elements-lanthanoids-and-actinoids","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"is